**Read:** [Electron-excited X-ray microanalysis by EDS at 50 — Newbury and Ritchie](../sources/NIST-Quantitative-EDS.lingua#r_7XJDCAH01NX65F6Q5YY3CBFTCF), the complete quantitative compositional-mapping treatment; [Guide to Thin Section Microscopy — Raith, Raase, and Reinhardt](../sources/Guide-to-Thin-Section-Microscopy.lingua#r_2MH30T4MGW8YMVP9SA3Z7XCDSD), the full imaging/context and measurement sections; [Modeling Metamorphic Rocks Using Equilibrium Thermodynamics — Lanari and Duesterhoeft](../sources/Metamorphic-Modeling-Lanari-Duesterhoeft.lingua#r_08B2X0N88PTC51CSQPZAGWAQV8), the full relevant domain/model interpretation account.

A compositional map records signals or derived composition across pixels under a measurement and processing method. Distinguish raw counts, background/corrected intensity, normalized composition, mineral classification, and a derived property map. Visually similar color scales can represent different quantities or transformations.

Pixel pitch, interaction volume, dwell, drift, detection, preparation, and segmentation affect resolution and interpretation. Adjacent pixels can be correlated and can mix boundaries or fine inclusions. Resampling or smoothing cannot create independent chemical observations at a smaller physical scale.

Connect maps with optical/structural images, spot analyses, section orientation, and specimen history. A region chosen as an equilibrium domain needs a geological argument. A perfectly segmented grain may still contain growth zones representing different events or chemical states.

**Work:** reconstruct a published map from its full methods and calibration account. Identify the raw and derived stages, evaluate a boundary/mixed-pixel example, and compare a classification with independent spot or structural observations. Repeat a consequential derived result under a plausible alternative segmentation or background choice and explain the geological effect.

Read the entire quantitative-mapping paper discussion and relevant microscopy/thermodynamic methods. Preserve original data, processing, scales, units, versions, and uncertainty in your study workspace. The next bulk-composition step relates these local domains to the material represented by an entire analysis.

**Continue:** [Keep bulk chemistry, mineral modes, and calculated norms distinct](Bulk-composition-modal-data-and-compositional-basis.lingua#r_5VR788G2X2J6VYBART77S5X1HQ).

